WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.962
The final set of topics in this course will cover some command-line tools you can use to get information and potentially troubleshoot situations.

00:07.962 --> 00:22.857
The first tool: ping. Ping is used to see if we can reach a destination. For example, in this command prompt I will type ping (space) www.learnkey.com and press Enter.

00:22.857 --> 00:38.190
We get four attempts to connect and four responses. This looks good. If we were to get a destination host unreachable message, that would signal a connectivity problem.

00:38.190 --> 00:50.410
When you need more than four responses from a ping command, you can run ping with a -t option, as I will do here, ping -t www.learnkey.com.

00:50.410 --> 01:03.805
This will keep pinging a location until I press Ctrl+C to stop the ping. And, if you want to ping an address a specified number of times,

01:03.805 --> 01:27.371
you can do so using the ping -n command, as I will do here through typing ping (space) -n, let's go 8 times, same website, I'll press Enter. Eight times.

01:27.371 --> 01:36.706
The latter two pings are often used when you need a larger sample size of pings due to some volatility in a network connection.

